import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.D66Xq0Kv.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Importing from Coolify, Dokploy, Heroku and Render","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/platforms.md","filePath":"guide/platforms.md"}'),s={name:"guide/platforms.md"};function i(n,e,d,h,c,p){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="importing-from-coolify-dokploy-heroku-and-render" tabindex="-1">Importing from Coolify, Dokploy, Heroku and Render <a class="header-anchor" href="#importing-from-coolify-dokploy-heroku-and-render" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Importing from Coolify, Dokploy, Heroku and Render&quot;">​</a></h1><p><strong>New project → Import from another platform</strong> reads what you run on Coolify, Dokploy, Heroku or Render and turns it into an Orbit project. Orbit shows the plan first and creates nothing until you confirm it. The other platform is never changed: it keeps serving until you move DNS.</p><h2 id="reads" tabindex="-1">What Orbit reads <a class="header-anchor" href="#reads" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What Orbit reads {#reads}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Give an API token with read access, and for Coolify or Dokploy the address of your installation. The token is used for one read and never stored. Variable values and database credentials are sealed on the server; the review shows variable names and kinds only.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Platform</th><th>Applications</th><th>Databases</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Coolify</td><td>Git repositories with their branch and build pack, registry images, exposed port, health check, start command, environment variables, FQDNs</td><td>PostgreSQL, MySQL and MariaDB, MongoDB, Redis and their forks</td><td>Compose applications are not read; import their file with the <a href="./compose">Compose import</a>. Persistent storages are noted.</td></tr><tr><td>Dokploy</td><td>GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket and plain Git sources, Docker images, build type, Dockerfile path, environment, domains, mounts, replicas</td><td>The project&#39;s PostgreSQL, MySQL, MariaDB, MongoDB and Redis services</td><td>Compose services are noted but not read.</td></tr><tr><td>Heroku</td><td>Apps, each formation type as its own application with its command, config vars, custom domains</td><td>Heroku Postgres and Heroku Redis add-ons, through their config vars</td><td>Apps built with buildpacks have no source Orbit can read: give an image reference in the review, or connect the repository&#39;s provider and set the source after creating. Container-stack apps point at <code>registry.heroku.com</code>, which needs a registry credential.</td></tr><tr><td>Render</td><td>Web services, private services, background workers, cron jobs and static sites, with repository, branch, Docker or native build, commands, health check, environment variables, custom domains</td><td>PostgreSQL and Redis instances</td><td>Root directories and attached disks are noted. Environment groups are not read.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="review" tabindex="-1">The review <a class="header-anchor" href="#review" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The review {#review}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every application and database appears as a proposal. Pick the platform project when there are several, give the Orbit project a name and a server, then adjust:</p><ul><li><strong>Names, process and port.</strong> Web applications need the port they listen on; Heroku and Render pass it through <code>PORT</code>, and so does Orbit.</li><li><strong>Source.</strong> Repositories on GitHub, GitLab or Bitbucket become Git sources, built from the repository root with the Dockerfile there, or with the buildpack strategy when the platform built without one. Images are deployed as they are. Anything else needs an image reference.</li><li><strong>Variables.</strong> They keep their values. A variable whose value is one of the platform&#39;s database URLs or hosts becomes a reference to the new database, so it follows the new credentials. Names Orbit does not accept, such as lowercase ones, are left out and listed.</li><li><strong>Domains.</strong> Custom domains are registered on the application without changing DNS. The Domains tab shows the records to set when you are ready to switch.</li><li><strong>What does not carry over.</strong> Volumes and mounts, since applications have no persistent storage on Orbit; subdirectories and Dockerfiles outside the repository root; Compose stacks; instance counts, which start at one; databases of engines Orbit does not manage, such as ClickHouse.</li></ul><p>Blocking notes keep an item out until it is fixed or unticked. Untick anything that should stay behind.</p><h2 id="create" tabindex="-1">Creating <a class="header-anchor" href="#create" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Creating {#create}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Confirming creates the project, then the databases, then each application with its variables, and registers the domains. Each step shows its progress and any failure, and the project opens like any other.</p><p>Databases start empty on the version Orbit runs. Bring the data with <strong>Import</strong> on each database&#39;s page, from a dump or from the old database&#39;s URL, as described in <a href="./backups#import">Backups and recovery</a>. Switch DNS when the applications answer with their data, and remove the token on the other platform if you created one for this.</p><p>The import is kept for 24 hours so you can create more applications from the same read; after that, read the platform again.</p>',13)])])}const u=t(s,[["render",i]]);export{m as __pageData,u as default};
